window.NOID_DOCS_INDEX=[{"t":"Welcome to No1d Swap","u":"","s":[{"h":"Welcome to No1d Swap","a":"","x":"No1d Swap is a desktop app for Windows and macOS that lets two people swap (Parano1d) for (Ethereum) directly—with no bridge, no wrapped token, no liquidity pool, and no middleman. A hash-time-locked contract on each chain holds the coins. Neither contract has an owner, an admin key, a pause button, or a protocol fee. Either both people receive what they agreed on, or both get their own coins back automatically once the deadlines pass. Read before swapping real funds: No1d Swap is early software built for two people who have a reason to trust each other, and for small amounts only. Start with Is No1d Swap Right for You Today? and Limits & Risks."},{"h":"At a Glance","a":"at-a-glance","x":"Detail Mainnet Sepolia (Testnet) Max per swap 2,500 and 0.5 20 and 0.01 Minimum swap 5 5 Protocol fee 0% (network fees only) 0% (network fees only) Typical time ~50 minutes from funding to settled ~50 minutes Refund time (if stopped) : ~2 h 20 min · : ~6 h 45 min : ~1 h 30 min · : ~3 h 5 min Bob's claim window after the reveal at least ~75 min only ~5 min (stay online) Supported OS Windows (x64) · macOS (Apple Silicon) Windows (x64) · macOS (Apple Silicon) Parano1d has no test network. Even when you choose Ethereum Sepolia, the side is always real Parano1d mainnet . If you want to test the app with zero coins and zero setup, use the built-in Practice Swap first."},{"h":"What No1d Swap Is—and Isn't","a":"what-no1d-swap-isand-isnt","x":"It is a self-custodial atomic swap app between two known counterparties. It is not an exchange, order book, broker, or custodial wallet. It does not set prices. You and the other person agree on the amounts elsewhere (for example, in chat or on a call), then enter those exact amounts into the app. It has not had an outside audit yet. Every security review so far was done internally by the project. Keep amounts small."},{"h":"Where to Start","a":"where-to-start","x":"Decide if it fits your swap: Read Is No1d Swap Right for You Today? (2 minutes). Understand the mechanics: Skim How It Works so the order of steps and deadlines makes sense. Install safely: Follow Download & Verify the Installer to verify the signed release notice and file hash before opening the installer. Rehearse with fake coins: Run the 5-minute Practice Swap from the app menu. Run your first swap: Follow the Swap Overview & Two-Person Checklist together with your counterparty."},{"h":"Official Links & Pinned Identifiers","a":"official-links-pinned-identifiers","x":"Source repository: github.com/00Para/noid-swap Updates on X: @No1dSwap Mainnet Ethereum contract (HTLCPoseidonV2): 0x4e364cC86EdBF307CC6F336F3a72e1a24d284D70 Pinned contract code hash: 0xf510d2a368c730339fa7cf5e023bcf2429d5732793f043e03025780bb7043ff3 Pinned Ed25519 release public key: Thg9nhwwvqoKG4yOqiZM/PBeqT5LcrFFa7cKAHuqYxk= Never trust contract addresses or installer hashes from screenshots or chat messages—always cross-check them using Download & Verify the Installer and Verify the Smart Contracts."},{"h":"Support the Project (Voluntary Donations)","a":"support-the-project-voluntary-donations","x":"No1d Swap charges 0% protocol fees. If you find it useful and want to support ongoing development and an independent external security audit, voluntary donations are welcome: o1epu2t45yg2a87xfvtzd3yuha8vejj9k4s762n89dwun3cp67k7nqkt2ued Donations are strictly optional and never required to use the software."}]},{"t":"Is No1d Swap Right for You Today?","u":"before-you-start/is-this-right-for-you/","s":[{"h":"Is No1d Swap Right for You Today?","a":"","x":"Before you install anything, take two minutes to check whether No1d Swap fits what you want to do. The app is designed to fail safely—but atomic swaps still have real requirements that an exchange hides from you."},{"h":"Quick Pre-Flight Checklist","a":"quick-pre-flight-checklist","x":"No1d Swap is a good fit today if all five of these are true: You already have a counterparty you have a reason to trust. There is no order book or matching engine. More importantly, Parano1d mining is currently concentrated enough that an seller should only swap with someone they have a reason to trust (see below). Your swap fits inside the launch caps. On Ethereum mainnet, the app enforces a hard limit of 5 to 2,500 and at most 0.5 per swap. Only swap amounts you can afford to lose while the software is young and unaudited. You have ~1 hour where both computers can stay on and online. A normal swap takes about 50 minutes from funding to finish. If you are selling (Bob), your computer must be online during the ~75-minute claim window after Alice reveals the secret. You can run the Parano1d wallet app locally. No1d Swap talks to your own synced Parano1d wallet app on 127.0.0.1:9601—it does not ask a third-party server to handle your wallet. You are using regular Ethereum accounts (EOAs), not smart-contract wallets. Ethereum payout addresses must be standard externally owned accounts (like a normal hardware wallet or MetaMask account) that accept plain transfers cleanly."},{"h":"The Two Roles: Alice vs. Bob","a":"the-two-roles-alice-vs-bob","x":"In every swap, the roles are fixed by which coin you are selling: Role You sell You receive Key responsibility Alice Locks first (longer deadline ~6 h 45 min if stopped). Must reveal before the cutoff. Bob Sends the invitation first. Exposed to Parano1d miner concentration and must stay online to claim within ~75 min after Alice reveals. Asymmetric risk: If you are selling (Alice), you are not exposed to Parano1d majority-miner attacks—your worst case if Bob disappears is waiting ~6 h 45 min for your to unlock. If you are selling (Bob), you carry both the online-claim requirement and the Parano1d miner-concentration risk."},{"h":"Why Counterparty Trust Still Matters Today","a":"why-counterparty-trust-still-matters-today","x":"Even though smart contracts remove custody risk, Parano1d mining is currently concentrated: Measurements from chain block headers (2026-11-08) showed a single mining address producing 45% to 63% of daily blocks over 20 days. If the seller (Alice) also controls more than 50% of Parano1d's hashrate, she could take Bob's and then stall Bob's claim until the deadline expires—taking both sides. No timeout length can defeat a majority miner. What the app does: Before Bob locks , his app reads recent Parano1d block headers (both 24-hour and 6-hour windows): Above 35%: Bob must explicitly acknowledge the measured share in his approval. Above 65%: The app hard-blocks new locks—nobody can override it until both windows drop back to 65% or lower. Read the full explanation in Limits & Risks."},{"h":"When You Should Wait Instead","a":"when-you-should-wait-instead","x":"Hold off on a real swap if: You want to trade with an anonymous stranger for a large amount. You are on a laptop that you need to close and put in a bag mid-swap (closing a Mac lid on battery can still sleep the machine). You need an instant swap—chain finality alone takes ~20 minutes on Ethereum and ~10 minutes on Parano1d. You want to swap USDC, BTC, or another asset—those are not enabled in the mainnet desktop app today. Want to see how the app feels without risking anything? Jump straight to Try a 5-Minute Practice Swap."}]},{"t":"How It Works","u":"before-you-start/how-it-works/","s":[{"h":"How It Works","a":"","x":"No1d Swap uses hash-time-locked contracts (HTLCs) on both Parano1d and Ethereum. You don't need to know cryptography to use the app, but understanding four core concepts makes every screen—and every safety warning—immediately obvious."},{"h":"Four Words to Know","a":"four-words-to-know","x":"Secret (s): A random 32-byte number generated on Alice's computer when the swap starts. Nobody else knows it at first. Hash-time lock: A smart contract lock with exactly two ways out: Before the deadline: Opens for the counterparty when the secret is shown. After the deadline: Returns the coins to the person who locked them. Reveal: The moment Alice claims Bob's on Ethereum by submitting the secret. Once that transaction lands on Ethereum, the secret is public—and Bob's app reads it right off the chain. Deadline: A hard time or block height after which coins go back to their original owner, so funds can never stay locked forever."},{"h":"The Swap in One Picture","a":"the-swap-in-one-picture","x":"Alice sells . Bob sells . Notice what never happens: Neither person ever holds both coins at the same time (unless Bob goes offline and misses his claim window). No bridge, relayer, custodian, or team multisig ever touches the coins."},{"h":"Why the NOID Seller Always Goes First","a":"why-the-noid-seller-always-goes-first","x":"On most chains, either side could lock first. On Parano1d, the order is fixed for an elegant cryptographic reason: Parano1d uses zero-knowledge proofs, not signatures. When you spend a coin, you prove in zero knowledge that you know the secret behind its address A = Addr(s)—without ever putting s on the blockchain. Because Parano1d never reveals s, Ethereum has to be the reveal chain. Whoever owns s at the start must claim on Ethereum to make s public. And because A = Addr(s) is already a hashlock, the Parano1d contract (htlc.pruv) doesn't need extra opcodes—it compiles to a single instruction. On Ethereum, HTLCPoseidonV2 computes Addr(s) directly in Solidity/Yul. Both chains lock on the exact same 32-byte value A, so no slow off-chain zero-knowledge equivalence proof is needed for swaps. That single design choice locks in the entire sequence: Alice ( seller) creates the secret, locks first with the longer deadline, and reveals s on Ethereum. Bob ( seller) locks second with the shorter deadline, reads s from Alice's Ethereum claim, and claims last."},{"h":"Why the Deadlines Are Staggered","a":"why-the-deadlines-are-staggered","x":"The two deadlines are deliberately unequal so both sides stay safe when everything stops mid-swap: Ethereum's deadline (T_reveal) always expires well before Parano1d's deadline (D). If Alice vanishes after Bob locks , Bob gets his back (~2 h 20 min after his lock) without Alice ever learning a way to steal it. If Alice reveals near the end of her window, her app refuses to reveal in the final 30 minutes—preventing a dangerous race where she publishes s right as Bob's refund activates. Whenever Alice does reveal in time, Bob is guaranteed at least ~75 minutes to claim his and wait for Parano1d finality (19 blocks) before Alice's refund opens."},{"h":"What Happens If Something Goes Wrong?","a":"what-happens-if-something-goes-wrong","x":"Scenario Outcome for Alice () Outcome for Bob () Either person stops before locking If Alice didn't fund yet, nothing moved. If she funded, returns after ~6 h 45 min (minus fees). Nothing locked; loses nothing. Bob never locks unlocks back to Alice after ~6 h 45 min (minus fees). Nothing locked; loses nothing. Alice never reveals s unlocks back to Alice after ~6 h 45 min (minus fees). refunds to Bob after ~2 h 20 min (minus gas). Alice tries to reveal too late App refuses in the last 30 min before T_reveal → safely becomes \"Alice never reveals.\" refunds to Bob after ~2 h 20 min. Bob is offline > 75 min after Alice reveals Alice keeps the and can reclaim the after D. Bob loses the . Stay online after locking! Good to know for Bob: Once Alice reveals s on Ethereum, anyone who has the public swap files can broadcast Bob's claim—and the contract forces the payout to go only to Bob's address. See Emergency Recovery & Second-Computer Close if your main computer ever loses power or internet mid-swap."}]},{"t":"Download & Verify the Installer","u":"getting-started/install-and-verify/","s":[{"h":"Download & Verify the Installer","a":"","x":"No1d Swap is distributed on the GitHub Releases page as a native installer for Windows and macOS. Because the project has no company name and no commercial code-signing certificate, verifying the cryptographic release signature before installing is essential. Never skip verification. A compromised GitHub account could replace both an installer and the SHA-256 hashes printed in the release notes. Only a valid Ed25519 signature from the project's offline release key proves an installer is authentic."},{"h":"Step 1: Download the Three Release Files","a":"step-1-download-the-three-release-files","x":"Go to the No1d Swap Releases page and download three files from the latest release into the same folder (for example, Downloads): Your installer: Windows (x64): No1d-Swap-<version>-win-x64.exe macOS (Apple Silicon): No1d-Swap-<version>-mac-arm64.dmg release.json — the manifest listing the version, oldest safe version, and each installer's SHA-256 hash. release.json.sig — the Ed25519 signature over the exact bytes of release.json."},{"h":"Step 2: Check the Pinned Release Public Key","a":"step-2-check-the-pinned-release-public-key","x":"Every release is signed with one offline Ed25519 key. Its public key is compiled into the app (desktop/lib/releasekey.js in the repository): Thg9nhwwvqoKG4yOqiZM/PBeqT5LcrFFa7cKAHuqYxk= Cross-check this string against desktop/lib/releasekey.js in the repository and independent project channels (such as @No1dSwap on X)."},{"h":"Step 3: Verify Signature & Installer SHA-256","a":"step-3-verify-signature-installer-sha-256","x":"You need Node.js 21 or later (Node 22 recommended) to run the repository's verification script, or you can use our one-command verifier. Verify release.json.sig against the pinned public key: node /path/to/noid-swap/desktop/scripts/release-key.js verify \\ --pub Thg9nhwwvqoKG4yOqiZM/PBeqT5LcrFFa7cKAHuqYxk= \\ --in ~/Downloads/release.json \\ --sig ~/Downloads/release.json.sig You must see OK. If you see anything else, stop and delete the download. Compute the installer's SHA-256: shasum -a 256 ~/Downloads/No1d-Swap-*-mac-arm64.dmg Compare the hash character-for-character with the matching filename inside release.json: cat ~/Downloads/release.json (Optional extra check) Verify GitHub build provenance: gh attestation verify ~/Downloads/No1d-Swap-*-mac-arm64.dmg --repo 00Para/noid-swap Verify release.json.sig against the pinned public key: node C:\\path\\to\\noid-swap\\desktop\\scripts\\release-key.js verify ` --pub Thg9nhwwvqoKG4yOqiZM/PBeqT5LcrFFa7cKAHuqYxk= ` --in \"$HOME\\Downloads\\release.json\" ` --sig \"$HOME\\Downloads\\release.json.sig\" You must see OK. If you see anything else, stop and delete the download. Compute the installer's SHA-256: Get-FileHash \"$HOME\\Downloads\\No1d-Swap-*-win-x64.exe\" -Algorithm SHA256 Compare the hash (case-insensitive) with the matching filename inside release.json: Get-Content \"$HOME\\Downloads\\release.json\" (Optional extra check) Verify GitHub build provenance: gh attestation verify \"$HOME\\Downloads\\No1d-Swap-*-win-x64.exe\" --repo 00Para/noid-swap If the signature fails or the SHA-256 does not match release.json, do not open the installer. Report it immediately using the instructions in SECURITY.md."},{"h":"Step 4: Install Past OS Warnings","a":"step-4-install-past-os-warnings","x":"Because the installers are not signed with an Apple Developer or Microsoft Authenticode certificate, your operating system will warn you the first time you open them. Only proceed after Step 3 passed. Open the verified .dmg and drag No1d Swap into Applications. Try launching No1d Swap once—macOS will block it as an unidentified developer. Open System Settings → Privacy & Security, scroll down to the Security section, and click Open Anyway. Confirm with your Mac password or Touch ID. Run the verified .exe installer. When Windows protected your PC (SmartScreen) appears, click More info. Click Run anyway to complete the per-user installation."},{"h":"How Automatic Update Checks Work","a":"how-automatic-update-checks-work","x":"Once installed, No1d Swap checks for new releases by downloading release.json and release.json.sig from GitHub and verifying the signature against the compiled-in public key: It never auto-downloads or auto-installs executables. You always verify and install updates yourself. Invalid signatures are rejected. If a release has a missing or bad signature, the app warns you not to install it. Safety cutoff (oldest_safe_version): If a critical flaw is discovered in an older release, a signed release.json can raise oldest_safe_version. The app will refuse to start new swaps on older versions—while never blocking refunds, claims, or sweeps for swaps already in progress."},{"h":"Next Step","a":"next-step","x":"Continue to First Launch, Recovery Code & Node Setup."}]},{"t":"First Launch, Recovery Code & Node Setup","u":"getting-started/first-launch-and-recovery/","s":[{"h":"First Launch, Recovery Code & Node Setup","a":"","x":"When you open No1d Swap for the first time, it sets up a private vault in your operating system's key store and gives you a paper recovery code. Do not rush past this screen."},{"h":"1. Save Your Recovery Code (First Launch)","a":"1-save-your-recovery-code-first-launch","x":"On first start, No1d Swap opens a dedicated, isolated secret window showing your recovery code. Write your recovery code on paper before you ever deposit coins. What it protects you from: If your OS key store is wiped, corrupted, or the desktop app refuses to start mid-swap, your recovery code + the swap's run folder let you unlock and finish the swap by hand with swap-cli. Who can use it: Anyone who has both your recovery code and your run folders can access your swap keys. Store it on paper, not in cloud notes or screenshots."},{"h":"Where Your Run Folders Live","a":"where-your-run-folders-live","x":"No1d Swap stores each swap's state in a private, owner-only data folder (uninstalling the app never deletes it): ~/Library/Application Support/No1d Swap/runs/<swap-id>/ %LOCALAPPDATA%\\No1d Swap\\runs\\<swap-id>\\"},{"h":"2. Prepare Your Parano1d Node","a":"2-prepare-your-parano1d-node","x":"No1d Swap never trusts a remote server for your Parano1d chain view by default—it connects to the local Parano1d wallet app running on your own computer. Open the Parano1d wallet app and wait until it is fully synced to the chain tip. Confirm its local RPC server is listening on 127.0.0.1:9601 (the default). Keep the Parano1d wallet app open for the entire duration of any real swap. Just want to try the app first? You do not need the Parano1d wallet app, a synced node, or any coins to run the built-in Practice Swap."},{"h":"Optional: Second Parano1d Node (Recommended for Mainnet)","a":"optional-second-parano1d-node-recommended-for-mainnet","x":"For Ethereum mainnet swaps—especially if you are Bob (selling )—No1d Swap uses a two-node cross-check so a single lying or stalled node cannot trick you about chain tips, lock status, or miner concentration. Configure your node endpoints in Settings before starting a mainnet swap."},{"h":"3. Prepare Your Ethereum Wallet & Funds","a":"3-prepare-your-ethereum-wallet-funds","x":"No1d Swap creates a fresh, temporary swap wallet (one wallet + one Ethereum key) for every swap so you never paste your main wallet's seed phrase or private key into the app. Before starting a real swap, have ready: Your regular Ethereum wallet (e.g., MetaMask or hardware wallet) on Ethereum mainnet (or Sepolia for a test run) with: If you are Bob (selling ): Your swap amount + a small buffer for Ethereum gas (lock + optional refund/sweep). If you are Alice (selling ): Your own Ethereum payout address (an EOA—not a smart-contract wallet), plus a small amount of ready for the swap key to pay the reveal transaction gas (~689,000 gas). Your regular Parano1d wallet with: If you are Alice (selling ): Your swap amount + a small buffer for the funding transaction fee (~0.009 ). If you are Bob (selling ): Your own Parano1d payout address (o1...) where the claimed will be sent. Address-history safety net: No1d Swap seals a local history of payout addresses you've used before. When you enter a payout address, the form tells you whether it's new, previously used, or changed from your last swap—helping you catch clipboard typos before you deposit."},{"h":"4. Power & Sleep Settings","a":"4-power-sleep-settings","x":"While coins are sitting in a contract, No1d Swap asks your OS to keep the computer awake. However: On macOS laptops: Closing the lid on battery can still put the Mac to sleep. Leave your laptop plugged in with the lid open until the swap says Done. On Windows: Power outages can interrupt pending folder writes. Keep your machine plugged in and avoid force-restarting mid-swap."},{"h":"Next Step","a":"next-step","x":"Before touching real coins, take 5 minutes to run a Practice Swap."}]},{"t":"Try a 5-Minute Practice Swap","u":"getting-started/practice-swap/","s":[{"h":"Try a 5-Minute Practice Swap","a":"","x":"Before running a swap with real coins, walk through the built-in Practice Swap. You'll see the exact cards, check lines, approvals, deposits, and waiting states of a real swap—without touching any network, node, key store, or wallet."},{"h":"How to Open It","a":"how-to-open-it","x":"Launch No1d Swap. Open Practice swap from the app menu, or press the Practice swap button on your list of swaps (the first-start guide offers it too). Pick a role—Alice (selling ) or Bob (selling )—and choose one of the guided scenarios. Completely offline and isolated: Practice Swap runs against a local script that plays both the other person and both blockchains. It needs no Parano1d node, no Ethereum RPC, no internet, and no coins, and it writes nothing to disk."},{"h":"What You Will Experience","a":"what-you-will-experience","x":"Every practice screen displays a clear Practice banner at the top so you can never confuse it with a real swap. Every address and amount is intentionally synthetic—nothing looks like a real Parano1d or Ethereum address, and you'll click a \"Pretend\" deposit button instead of sending real funds. There are three scenarios, each playable as Alice or as Bob: A swap that goes through — from the invitation and the check lines through funding, lock, reveal, claim and the leftover sweep. The other side never locks — Alice's comes back after the deadline; Bob's swap simply never starts. Alice never reveals — both locks, then the reveal is refused as too late, Bob's is refunded and Alice takes her back after the deadline. Each wait ends after a few seconds (or press Skip the wait), and the real time of each step is shown beside it. Try running Practice Swap once as Alice and once as Bob—it takes about five minutes total and makes your first real swap feel completely familiar."},{"h":"Safety Rule While Real Swaps Are Active","a":"safety-rule-while-real-swaps-are-active","x":"Practice Swap can never hide a real swap that needs your attention: If a real swap currently Needs you, the app refuses to start a new practice session until you handle it. If a real swap needs you while you're already inside a practice session, a real alarm (\"A real swap needs you\") immediately appears above the practice banner with a one-click \"Leave the practice and open it\" button."},{"h":"Next Step","a":"next-step","x":"Ready for a real swap (or a small Sepolia rehearsal)? Head to Swap Overview & Two-Person Checklist."}]},{"t":"Swap Overview & Two-Person Checklist","u":"swap-guide/overview-and-checklist/","s":[{"h":"Swap Overview & Two-Person Checklist","a":"","x":"A No1d Swap takes two people sitting at their screens together for the first few minutes, followed by roughly 50 minutes of automated waiting while both blockchains finalize each leg. Before either person sends a single coin, the app guides you through a short three-part handshake: Bob sends an invitation (amounts + Bob's two public addresses—zero secrets). Alice prepares the swap and sends back her public swap copy (alice.bundle.json—zero secrets). Both compare the 16-digit check line on a separate channel (call, Signal, etc.) and approve once. Only after both sides confirm the check line and approve does anyone deposit coins."},{"h":"Side-by-Side Flow","a":"side-by-side-flow","x":"Step Alice (sells → gets ) Bob (sells → gets ) 0. Agree on terms Agree on exact & amounts and network (Mainnet or Sepolia). Agree on exact & amounts and network. 1. Invitation Wait for Bob's invitation. Create a New swap as Bob → Save an invitation for Alice… (no1d-invitation.json) (or Copy invitation code, v0.1.8 and later) and send it to Alice. 2. Prepare Open Bob's invitation in New swap, double-check every field, enter your payout addresses, and click Prepare. (This starts the ~6-hour clock!) Wait for Alice's public swap file. 3. Swap copy Save your public swap copy (alice.bundle.json) and send it to Bob. Choose Alice's file… (alice.bundle.json); the app verifies it automatically. 4. Check line Read your 16-digit check line (xxxx xxxx xxxx xxxx) to Bob on another channel and confirm it matches. Compare your 16-digit check line with Alice's and confirm it matches. 5. Single approval Review rate, fees, and worst-case refund time → approve once. Review rate, fees, worst-case refund time, and Parano1d miner share → approve once. 6. Deposit Send (and a tiny gas reserve for reveal) to the swap's own deposit addresses shown on screen. Send (+ gas buffer) to the swap's own Ethereum key address shown on screen. 7. Automated execution App funds contract → waits for Bob's final lock → reveals s & takes → sweeps leftovers to your payout address. App checks Alice's final lock + miner share → locks → reads s after reveal → claims → sweeps leftovers to your payout address. Never send your main wallet seed, your recovery code, or *.swap.json to anyone. The only things that ever leave your computer are Bob's public invitation (no1d-invitation.json or no1dinv1:…) and Alice's public bundle (alice.bundle.json), neither of which contains any secret."},{"h":"Why the 16-Digit Check Line Matters","a":"why-the-16-digit-check-line-matters","x":"The check line is four groups of four digits (16 digits total) computed independently on both computers from the exact agreed swap terms—amounts, addresses, hashlock, contract address, and deadlines. Comparing the check line over a second channel (voice call, video call, or a separate messaging app) guarantees that: Neither side has a typo in the amounts or addresses. Nobody tampered with the invitation or bundle file in transit. Both computers are about to lock against the exact same contract and hashlock. If the check lines don't match digit for digit, stop and do not approve or deposit."},{"h":"Pre-Swap Quick Check (Both Sides)","a":"pre-swap-quick-check-both-sides","x":"Parano1d wallet app is open and fully synced (127.0.0.1:9601). Paper recovery code is written down safely. Both people are at their screens before Alice presses Prepare. Laptops are plugged in with lids open (closing a Mac lid on battery can sleep it). Testnet reminder: If you're doing a dry run on Sepolia, remember that is still real mainnet (capped at 20 / 0.01 Sepolia ), and Bob's claim window after the reveal is only about 5 minutes on Sepolia (75 on mainnet): Bob stays at his screen."},{"h":"Detailed Role Guides","a":"detailed-role-guides","x":"Selling ? Follow the Guide for Alice (Selling NOID). Selling ? Follow the Guide for Bob (Selling ETH). Want exact numbers first? Check Caps, Fees & Timelines."}]},{"t":"Guide for Alice (Selling NOID)","u":"swap-guide/alice-selling-noid/","s":[{"h":"Guide for Alice (Selling NOID)","a":"","x":"Follow this guide if you hold and want to receive . Because Parano1d spends coins with zero-knowledge proofs, you always generate the swap secret, lock first, and later reveal the secret on Ethereum to collect Bob's ."},{"h":"Before You Start","a":"before-you-start","x":"Make sure you have: The Parano1d wallet app open and fully synced on this computer (127.0.0.1:9601). Your to sell (between 5 and 2,500 on mainnet; 5 to 20 on Sepolia), plus a tiny extra amount (~0.01 ) for the funding fee. Your Ethereum payout address (must be a standard EOA wallet—not a smart-contract wallet). A small amount of in your regular wallet to fund the swap key's Ethereum gas for the reveal transaction (~689,000 gas). Bob online and ready—pressing Prepare starts the ~6-hour Parano1d deadline clock."},{"h":"Step 1: Open Bob's Invitation","a":"step-1-open-bobs-invitation","x":"Bob creates the swap first and sends you his invitation. Click New swap in No1d Swap and choose Alice (selling ). Load Bob's invitation: From a file: Click Open Bob's invitation… and select the no1d-invitation.json file Bob sent you. From a pasted code (v0.1.8 and later): Paste Bob's no1dinv1:… invitation code into the paste box and click Use code. The code's checksum catches a damaged paste, not a forged one, so the next step still matters. Double-check every auto-filled field: network (Mainnet vs. Sepolia), amount, amount, and Bob's addresses. Never trust an auto-fill blindly. Enter your Ethereum payout address (where your will be sent) and check the address-history note (new, used before, or changed). Review the rate, fees, usual time (~50 min), and worst-case refund time (~6 h 45 min) displayed on the form."},{"h":"Step 2: Press Prepare & Send Your Swap Copy to Bob","a":"step-2-press-prepare-send-your-swap-copy-to-bob","x":"Prepare starts the clock. Only click Prepare once Bob is at his computer and ready to proceed. Click Prepare. Behind the scenes, No1d Swap creates a fresh per-swap wallet, a fresh per-swap Ethereum key, a random 32-byte secret s (sealed in your vault), and the Parano1d contract opening. Click Save a copy for Bob… to save alice.bundle.json. This file contains only public swap terms and hashes—never your secret or keys. Send alice.bundle.json to Bob."},{"h":"Step 3: Verify the 16-Digit Check Line","a":"step-3-verify-the-16-digit-check-line","x":"Once Bob loads alice.bundle.json, both screens show a check line (four groups of four digits). Read your check line to Bob over a separate channel (phone call, Signal, etc.). Confirm that all 16 digits match exactly. Tick the confirmation that Bob's check line matches yours."},{"h":"Step 4: Approve Once","a":"step-4-approve-once","x":"Review the single approval box carefully. It summarizes: Exactly how much you are giving and how much you expect after fees. Your payout address (shown in full). The fee cap (default 1 gwei on Ethereum—you can adjust this if mainnet gas is higher). Type the confirmation amount, tick the boxes, and approve. One approval covers the automated funding, reveal, and leftover sweeps."},{"h":"Step 5: Deposit Into the Swap's Wallets","a":"step-5-deposit-into-the-swaps-wallets","x":"After approval, the card displays the swap's own deposit addresses (shown in full, with read-back verification when you copy them): Send the exact amount (+ funding fee buffer) from your Parano1d wallet app to the swap's deposit address, watching the countdown timer on screen. Send the small gas deposit shown on screen to the swap's Ethereum key so it can pay gas to reveal and claim Bob's later. Short deposit? If you accidentally send slightly too little, the app flags the exact shortfall so you can top it up cleanly—nothing is lost."},{"h":"Step 6: Let the App Finish Automatically","a":"step-6-let-the-app-finish-automatically","x":"Keep No1d Swap and your Parano1d wallet app open (and your laptop plugged in with the lid open). From here, every step runs automatically behind a dry run and a safety gate: funding: Your app locks your into the Parano1d contract and waits for finality (19 blocks). Waiting for Bob: Your app watches Ethereum until Bob locks the exact amount against your hashlock with enough time left, and waits for Ethereum finality (~19 minutes). Reveal & claim: Once Bob's lock is final, your app broadcasts the reveal transaction on Ethereum, publishing s and sending the to your payout address. Safety guard: If Ethereum gas spikes and your reveal gets stuck, the card lets you re-send at a higher fee cap. Cutoff protection: Your app automatically refuses to reveal inside the final 30 minutes before Bob's refund opens, so you can never leak s without getting paid. Leftover sweep: Any unused dust/gas remaining in your temporary swap wallets is automatically swept back to your payout addresses. The swap says Done only once the has arrived at your own payout address."},{"h":"What If Bob Never Locks?","a":"what-if-bob-never-locks","x":"If Bob disappears or never locks his : Your app waits until the Parano1d deadline D (~6 h 45 min after Prepare). Once D passes, your app automatically recovers your back to your payout address (minus network fees). Your secret s is never revealed."}]},{"t":"Guide for Bob (Selling ETH)","u":"swap-guide/bob-selling-eth/","s":[{"h":"Guide for Bob (Selling ETH)","a":"","x":"Follow this guide if you hold and want to receive . As Bob, you start the handshake by sending Alice an invitation, lock your after verifying Alice's lock is final, and claim the as soon as Alice reveals the secret on Ethereum. Two critical rules for Bob: Only swap with someone you have a reason to trust while Parano1d mining remains concentrated (see Limits & Risks). Stay online until the swap finishes. After Alice reveals the secret on Ethereum, your computer has at least ~75 minutes to claim your . If your computer is off or offline during that window, Alice can reclaim the after the deadline and keep your ."},{"h":"Before You Start","a":"before-you-start","x":"Make sure you have: The Parano1d wallet app open and fully synced on this computer (127.0.0.1:9601). Your to sell (up to 0.5 on mainnet; up to 0.01 on Sepolia), plus extra to cover the lock transaction (~584,000 gas) and a potential refund (~159,000 gas) if Alice stops. Your Parano1d payout address (o1...) where your claimed will be sent. Your Ethereum payout address (standard EOA) for sweeping back any leftover gas (or refund). Your computer plugged in with the lid open so it won't sleep mid-swap."},{"h":"Step 1: Create the Swap & Send an Invitation to Alice","a":"step-1-create-the-swap-send-an-invitation-to-alice","x":"Click New swap in No1d Swap and choose Bob (selling ). Choose the network (Ethereum Mainnet or Sepolia). Enter the agreed amount (5–2,500 on mainnet) and amount (up to 0.5 on mainnet). Enter your Parano1d payout address and Ethereum payout address (check the address-history indicator). Create the swap—the app generates a fresh per-swap Ethereum key for this swap. Send the invitation to Alice: As a file: Click Save an invitation for Alice… to save no1d-invitation.json and send it to Alice. As a copyable code (v0.1.8 and later): Click Copy invitation code (no1dinv1:…) and paste it into your chat with Alice. Both formats contain only public fields (network, contract, amounts, and your two public addresses)—zero secrets. Do not deposit yet! Wait until Alice sends back her swap file, you compare check lines, and you approve."},{"h":"Step 2: Load Alice's Swap Copy & Check the Check Line","a":"step-2-load-alices-swap-copy-check-the-check-line","x":"Alice presses Prepare and sends you alice.bundle.json. Click Choose Alice's file… on your swap card and pick alice.bundle.json. Your app verifies the contract code hash, hashlock, amounts, and deadlines automatically. Both screens display a 16-digit check line (xxxx xxxx xxxx xxxx). Compare the check line with Alice over a second channel (phone call, Signal, etc.) and confirm all 16 digits match."},{"h":"Step 3: Check Parano1d Miner Concentration & Approve Once","a":"step-3-check-parano1d-miner-concentration-approve-once","x":"Before you lock any , No1d Swap reads Parano1d's own block headers across both the last 24 hours (2,880 blocks) and last 6 hours (720 blocks) to measure the largest single mining address: Largest producer share (24 h or 6 h) What the app does 35% or lower Passes normally. Between 35% and 65% Shows the exact measured percentage inside your approval and asks you to explicitly accept it and confirm you have a reason to trust Alice. Above 65% Hard block. The approval is disabled and cannot be waived. The screen reopens automatically once both windows fall to 65% or lower. Review the rate, fees, worst-case refund time (~2 h 20 min), and miner share, type the confirmation amount, tick the boxes, and approve once."},{"h":"Step 4: Deposit ETH Into the Swap's Ethereum Key","a":"step-4-deposit-eth-into-the-swaps-ethereum-key","x":"After approval, the card displays this swap's Ethereum key address (in full, with clipboard read-back verification). Send the required amount (swap amount + gas reserve shown on screen) from your regular Ethereum wallet to that address. If your deposit is slightly short, the app flags the exact shortfall within seconds of the block so you can top it up."},{"h":"Step 5: Stay Online While the App Finishes Automatically","a":"step-5-stay-online-while-the-app-finishes-automatically","x":"Keep No1d Swap and your Parano1d wallet app open and online: Waiting for Alice's lock: Your app verifies Alice's contract is funded and final on Parano1d (19 blocks), re-checks miner concentration, and verifies enough time remains before D. lock: Your app locks your in HTLCPoseidonV2 on Ethereum and waits for finality (~19 minutes). Watching for Alice's reveal: Once Alice takes your , her transaction publishes the secret s in Ethereum's Claimed event. claim: Your app immediately reads s, builds a zero-knowledge proof, and broadcasts the close transaction to Parano1d—sending the (minus the ~0.0058 close fee) straight to your Parano1d payout address. Late-claim alarm: If time is getting tight before D - 19, the app raises a prominent late-claim alarm. Leftover sweep: Any unused gas remaining in the temporary swap key is automatically swept back to your Ethereum payout address. The swap says Done only once the has finalized in your own Parano1d address."},{"h":"What If Alice Disappears After You Lock ETH?","a":"what-if-alice-disappears-after-you-lock-eth","x":"If Alice never reveals s, your app waits until the 2-hour Ethereum reveal window (T_reveal) expires (~2 h 20 min after your lock, including finality). Once T_reveal passes, your app automatically refunds your back to your payout address. If the refund gets stuck due to rising Ethereum gas, the card lets you re-send the refund at a higher fee cap."},{"h":"What If Your Computer Dies After Alice Reveals?","a":"what-if-your-computer-dies-after-alice-reveals","x":"Don't panic! Once s is public on Ethereum, you can broadcast the close from a second computer (and even a friend with only the public swap files can broadcast it—the contract forces 100% of the payout to your Parano1d address). Follow Emergency Recovery & Second-Computer Close."}]},{"t":"Caps, Fees & Timelines","u":"swap-guide/caps-fees-and-timelines/","s":[{"h":"Caps, Fees & Timelines","a":"","x":"No1d Swap displays the exact exchange rate, estimated fees, usual duration, and worst-case refund time before your first click. This page collects all exact protocol constants and measured numbers from the app (ui/app/lib/launchfacts.js and swap-cli/src/params.rs)."},{"h":"Swap Caps","a":"swap-caps","x":"Limit Ethereum Mainnet (production) Ethereum Sepolia (sepolia) Maximum per swap 2,500 20 Maximum per swap 0.5 0.01 Minimum per swap 5 5 Project / protocol fee 0% 0% Why is the minimum 5 ? The swap tool sets each swap's close max_fee to at least 50,000 (0.05 ) and refuses one above 1% of the locked amount. Below 5 , 1% is less than 50,000 , so the app refuses smaller swaps. Caps are enforced in both the desktop settings layer and the underlying swap-cli profile."},{"h":"Network Fees","a":"network-fees","x":"You pay only blockchain network fees—No1d Swap takes no cut."},{"h":"Ethereum Gas (Measured vs. Safety Ceilings)","a":"ethereum-gas-measured-vs-safety-ceilings","x":"Step Who pays Measured gas App gas ceiling Lock (newLock) Bob ~583,853 700,000 Reveal / Claim (claim) Alice ~689,324 1,000,000 Refund (refund, abort path only) Bob ~158,608 200,000 Leftover Sweep Alice & Bob 21,000 21,000 Default fee cap: 1 gwei (configurable before approval; the app never broadcasts above your fee cap). Stuck transactions: If mainnet base fee spikes above your cap while a reveal or refund is waiting, the desktop card lets you re-send at a higher fee cap without restarting the swap. Fee loudness warning: If total estimated fees exceed 5% of your swap value, the form highlights it loudly so you don't accidentally overpay gas on a tiny swap."},{"h":"Parano1d (NOID) Fees","a":"parano1d-noid-fees","x":"1 = 1,000,000 (). Step Who pays Typical / minimum fee Notes Funding Alice ~9,000 (~0.009 ) Paid from Alice's swap wallet on top of the locked amount Close (Claim or Refund) Paid out of the locked ≥ 5,800 (0.0058 ), at most the swap's max_fee (at least 50,000 / 0.05 , at most 1% of the amount) Receiver gets locked amount − close fee Leftover Sweep Alice ~5,800 (0.0058 ) Sweeps any change left in the swap wallet"},{"h":"Timelines & Safety Windows (Ethereum Mainnet)","a":"timelines-safety-windows-ethereum-mainnet","x":"All times below assume normal block speeds (30 s/block on Parano1d, ~12 s/slot on Ethereum). Event / Window Mainnet Value Plain-English Meaning Usual total swap time ~50 minutes Measured from Alice's funding to both sides final (most of this is chain finality). Ethereum finality wait ~19 minutes (1,140 s) Both sides wait for Ethereum finality before moving to the next irreversible step. Parano1d finality wait 19 blocks (~9.5 min) Parano1d marks a block final 18 blocks after inclusion; claims must be included by D - 19. Parano1d deadline (D) 738 blocks after Prepare (~6 h) After D, Bob can no longer claim and Alice can recover her . Alice's reveal window (W_A) 7,200 seconds (2 hours) after Bob's lock Window during which HTLCPoseidonV2.claim() accepts Alice's secret. Alice's reveal safety cutoff (M_A) 30 minutes before T_reveal Alice's app refuses to reveal inside the last 30 min to avoid leaking s into a race with Bob's refund. Bob's guaranteed online claim window At least ~75 minutes Time Bob has to claim and reach finality after Alice reveals. Worst-case refund (Bob) ~2 h 20 min after Bob's lock Includes Ethereum finality + 2-hour reveal window. Worst-case refund (Alice) ~6 h 45 min after Prepare Time until Alice's is back in her own wallet if Bob never locks or Alice never reveals."},{"h":"Profile Comparison (Mainnet vs. Sepolia)","a":"profile-comparison-mainnet-vs-sepolia","x":"Parameter Mainnet (production) Sepolia (sepolia) D offset (Parano1d blocks) 738 blocks (~6 h) 302 blocks (~2.5 h) reveal window (W_A) 7,200 s (2 h) 4,200 s (70 min) Parano1d finality (B_final) 19 blocks 19 blocks Ethereum finality 1,140 s (~19 min) 1,140 s (~19 min) Usual end-to-end time ~50 min ~50 min Bob's guaranteed claim window after the reveal at least ~75 min only ~5 min: Bob must be online when Alice reveals Worst-case refund (Bob) ~2 h 20 min after his lock ~1 h 30 min after his lock Worst-case refund (Alice) ~6 h 45 min after Prepare ~3 h 5 min after Prepare Times are \"at usual block speed,\" never \"at most.\" If either chain produces blocks slower than usual, refund and finality times stretch proportionally."}]},{"t":"Limits & Risks","u":"security/limits-and-risks/","s":[{"h":"Limits & Risks","a":"","x":"No1d Swap removes bridges, custodians, and admin keys—but it does not remove every risk. This page mirrors the app's Limits and risks card in plain English so you know the exact boundaries before putting real funds on the line."},{"h":"The Three Ways to Lose Funds","a":"the-three-ways-to-lose-funds","x":""},{"h":"1. Bob Is Offline After Alice Reveals","a":"1-bob-is-offline-after-alice-reveals","x":"Who is exposed: Bob ( seller) only. What happens: Once Alice claims Bob's on Ethereum, the secret s is public and Bob's app has at least ~75 minutes to submit and finalize the claim before Parano1d deadline D. If Bob's computer is turned off, asleep, or disconnected for that entire window, Alice can reclaim the after D and keep the . How to stay safe: Plug your laptop in, leave the lid open, keep the Parano1d wallet app synced, and know how to run a second-computer close if your primary machine fails."},{"h":"2. Parano1d Majority-Miner Attack (Mining Concentration)","a":"2-parano1d-majority-miner-attack-mining-concentration","x":"Who is exposed: Bob ( seller) only. Alice is not exposed. What happens: If Alice (the seller) also controls more than 50% of Parano1d's mining hashrate, she can reveal s on Ethereum, collect Bob's , and then censor or reorg Bob's claim until D passes—taking both sides. No timeout length protects against a > 50% miner. Real-world state: Chain block-header measurements (2026-11-08) showed one mining address producing 45% to 63% of daily blocks over 20 days (52.7% on the last measured day). What Bob's app checks: Before Bob locks , his app reads recent Parano1d block headers across the last 24 hours (2,880 blocks) and 6 hours (720 blocks): 35%–65% in either window: Bob is shown the exact measured percentage and must explicitly accept it in his approval. Above 65% in either window: Hard block. Nobody can waive it; the approval screen reopens automatically once both windows fall back to ≤ 65%. Top two addresses > 50%: Displays a warning note. Why the screen is not a defense: A miner can split blocks across multiple fresh addresses, join a pool, or rent hashrate after Bob locks. Treat the gate as a smoke alarm, not a shield—sell only to someone you have a reason to trust."},{"h":"3. Unaudited Software & Unreviewed Hashlock Preimage Assumption","a":"3-unaudited-software-unreviewed-hashlock-preimage-assumption","x":"Who is exposed: Both Alice and Bob. What happens: Every code review, design review, formal model check, and chaos test in 00Para/noid-swap was conducted internally by the project—none came from an independent outside auditor or cryptographer. In addition, the swap hashlock relies on the preimage resistance of Parano1d's Addr(s) function (a single Poseidon2b permutation over GF(2¹²⁸)), which no external cryptographer has formally reviewed for cross-chain HTLC use. How to stay safe: Keep amounts small and strictly within the launch caps (0.5 / 2,500 )."},{"h":"Other Known Limitations (Non-Fatal, Built Into HTLCs)","a":"other-known-limitations-non-fatal-built-into-htlcs","x":"Even when nobody loses their coins, atomic swaps have economic quirks you should expect: Free option: After Alice locks , Bob can pause briefly before locking ; after Bob locks , Alice can wait before revealing s. Short windows and small amounts limit this, but it is inherent to HTLCs. Liquidity lock-up (griefing): If Bob disappears after Alice funds , Alice gets 100% of her back (minus network fees), but her funds are tied up for ~6 h 45 min. On-chain linkability: Anyone watching both chains can link Alice's lock and Bob's lock via the shared 32-byte value A = Addr(s) (even for swaps that refund). No smart-contract wallets on Ethereum: Ethereum payout addresses must be standard EOAs. A contract receiver that reverts on transfer would cause claim() to revert with the secret already exposed in calldata. First-seen mempool on Parano1d (close fee griefing): Anyone who knows s can broadcast the close to Bob at any fee up to the swap's max_fee (at least 50,000 / 0.05 , more when the network's fee estimate is high, and never above 1% of the swap). The still goes strictly to Bob, but Bob absorbs that fee."},{"h":"What Is Intentionally Disabled on Mainnet","a":"what-is-intentionally-disabled-on-mainnet","x":"To keep the mainnet surface small and well-tested, the desktop app refuses the following on Ethereum mainnet today: The Nostr offer board (test networks only) The legacy terminal web console Parano1d HTLC contract v2 (fixed-fee close — awaiting live hostile testing) The stock-wallet Parano1d variant (Derive(m, 0)) Legacy SHA-256 + RISC Zero equivalence-proof locks (Ethereum mainnet uses HTLCPoseidonV2) Bitcoin (btc/ signet prototype) and ERC-20 (HTLCERC20.sol)"},{"h":"Reporting a Vulnerability","a":"reporting-a-vulnerability","x":"If you find a bug that could put funds at risk, do not post details in a public GitHub issue. Open an issue on 00Para/noid-swap containing only: \"security, please open a private channel\" (or use GitHub's private vulnerability reporting if enabled). Include the commit/version, file and line number, attack scenario, and any reproduction test. See SECURITY.md in the repository for full details."}]},{"t":"Verify the Smart Contracts","u":"security/verify-the-contracts/","s":[{"h":"Verify the Smart Contracts","a":"","x":"No1d Swap never asks you to take our word on which contract holds your funds. Both the Ethereum contract and the per-swap Parano1d contract are immutable, ownerless, and independently verifiable."},{"h":"Live Contract Addresses","a":"live-contract-addresses","x":"Network Contract Address Explorer / Verification Ethereum Mainnet HTLCPoseidonV2 0x4e364cC86EdBF307CC6F336F3a72e1a24d284D70 Etherscan · Sourcify (exact match) Ethereum Sepolia HTLCPoseidonV2 0x48Ce4D6cf3c175737532017e82B9261b51F8E5eF Sepolia Etherscan Parano1d Mainnet Per-swap HashTimeLock Created fresh per swap from parano1d/contracts/htlc.pruv Rebuilt byte-for-byte locally by swap-cli before any lock"},{"h":"Pinned Ethereum Runtime Code Hash","a":"pinned-ethereum-runtime-code-hash","x":"On both Ethereum Mainnet and Sepolia, the keccak-256 hash of the deployed bytecode (eth_getCode) is: 0xf510d2a368c730339fa7cf5e023bcf2429d5732793f043e03025780bb7043ff3 No1d Swap compiles this hash directly into the swap tool and refuses to lock, claim, or refund against any contract whose on-chain bytecode produces a different hash."},{"h":"Properties of HTLCPoseidonV2","a":"properties-of-htlcposeidonv2","x":"No owner, no admin key, no multisig. Nobody can upgrade, pause, or touch locked . No protocol fee. 100% of locked goes either to Alice on claim() or back to Bob on refund(). No addrOf view function. Unlike the earlier HTLCPoseidon.sol prototype, HTLCPoseidonV2 deliberately omits any read-only addrOf(secret) view so no one is tempted to send a live secret to a remote RPC node via eth_call. Strict 68-byte calldata on claim(). Trailing garbage bytes revert with BadCalldataLength. Timelock checked before hashing. Expired claims revert cheaply before running the Poseidon2b permutation. Minimum gas guard (MIN_CLAIM_GAS). Prevents half-executed reverts from burning gas unnecessarily."},{"h":"How to Verify the Ethereum Contract Yourself","a":"how-to-verify-the-ethereum-contract-yourself","x":""},{"h":"1. Check Verified Source on Sourcify","a":"1-check-verified-source-on-sourcify","x":"Visit repo.sourcify.dev/1/0x4e364cC86EdBF307CC6F336F3a72e1a24d284D70 and compare the verified source files byte-for-byte with: eth/contracts/HTLCPoseidonV2.sol eth/contracts/Poseidon2b.sol in github.com/00Para/noid-swap."},{"h":"2. Independently Verify the Runtime Code Hash","a":"2-independently-verify-the-runtime-code-hash","x":"Using Foundry's cast (or any Ethereum RPC + keccak256): cast code 0x4e364cC86EdBF307CC6F336F3a72e1a24d284D70 --rpc-url <YOUR_MAINNET_RPC> \\ | cast keccak The output must equal: 0xf510d2a368c730339fa7cf5e023bcf2429d5732793f043e03025780bb7043ff3"},{"h":"How the Parano1d Contract Works (htlc.pruv)","a":"how-the-parano1d-contract-works-htlcpruv","x":"There is no shared contract address on Parano1d. Instead, every swap generates its own dedicated HashTimeLock contract instance from parano1d/contracts/htlc.pruv (which compiles to 1 of 16 instructions) parameterized with: claim_authority = A = Addr(s) (the 32-byte hashlock shared with Ethereum) claim_recipient = Bob's pinned Parano1d payout address refund_recipient = Alice's address in the swap terms (in the desktop app, the swap's own wallet, which then sweeps to her payout address) deadline = D (block height) max_fee = at least 50,000 μNOID (0.05 ) and at most 1% of swap amount Before Bob locks a single wei of , his app rebuilds the exact Parano1d contract definition locally, checks its root hash against Alice's on-chain lock, confirms claim_recipient is Bob's own address, and verifies the lock is final on Parano1d. Never fund parano1d/contracts/htlc.params.example.json: That repository fixture uses the public test secret 00 01 … 1f and exists strictly for automated testing."}]},{"t":"Local Security & Privacy","u":"security/privacy-and-local-security/","s":[{"h":"Local Security & Privacy","a":"","x":"Understanding what No1d Swap protects on your computer—and what it cannot protect against—helps you keep your setup clean and safe."},{"h":"How Your Secrets Are Stored","a":"how-your-secrets-are-stored","x":"Per-swap isolated wallets: For every new swap, No1d Swap creates a brand-new temporary Parano1d wallet and a brand-new temporary Ethereum key. You never enter your main wallet's seed phrase or private key into No1d Swap. OS key store vault: Swap secrets and temporary keys are sealed with AES-256-GCM using keys protected by your operating system's native key store (macOS Keychain / Windows DPAPI via Electron safeStorage). Dedicated secret window: Your paper recovery code is shown inside an isolated, hardened window served over a private no1d://secret/ scheme with strict Content Security Policy and zero external network access. No secrets on command lines or in logs: Passphrases and private keys are passed over stdin pipes or memory—never via command-line arguments where other processes could read them."},{"h":"What the OS Key Store Protects—and What It Doesn't","a":"what-the-os-key-store-protectsand-what-it-doesnt","x":"Threat Protected? Why Another user account on the same PC Yes Data folders are owner-only (0700 on macOS, owner/SYSTEM/Administrators ACL on Windows) and key store entries are per-user. A stolen hard drive or copied run folder Yes Sealed files (*.swap.json, payout-history.sealed) cannot be opened without your OS key store or paper recovery code. Clipboard typos / swapped payout addresses Mitigated Addresses are shown in full, read back after copying, and cross-checked against your sealed payout-address history. Malware running as your own user account No Any malicious program running under your login can read your OS key store, modify local files, or forge address history—just as it can attack any desktop wallet. Keep your paper recovery code separate from your run folders. Anyone who obtains both your recovery code and a swap's run folder can derive that swap's passphrase offline."},{"h":"Desktop Hardening Built Into the App","a":"desktop-hardening-built-into-the-app","x":"The desktop shell enforces strict Electron security controls on every launch: Electron fuses enabled at build time; remote debugging, proxy, and host-resolver CLI switches are refused at startup. Runtime manifest integrity check: On every packaged launch, the app verifies its own runtime manifest; a tampered installation stops with \"This installation is damaged: reinstall it.\" Direct proxy & WebRTC hardening: Every session sets proxy: direct and disable_non_proxied_udp. Single-instance lock & run locks: Prevents two windows from racing over the same swap files. Owner-only data folder enforcement: Verifies folder permissions before opening the vault."},{"h":"Privacy: What Is Public vs. Private","a":"privacy-what-is-public-vs-private","x":""},{"h":"What Stays Private on Your Computer","a":"what-stays-private-on-your-computer","x":"Your recovery code and vault secret Your temporary swap wallet keys Your swap secret s (private to Alice until the moment she claims on Ethereum) Your payout address history (payout-history.sealed)"},{"h":"What Is Public (On-Chain or Shared With Counterparty)","a":"what-is-public-on-chain-or-shared-with-counterparty","x":"Between Alice and Bob: Amounts, network, contract address, your swap payout addresses, and the hashlock A = Addr(s). On-chain linkability: Both Ethereum (HTLCPoseidonV2) and Parano1d (htlc.pruv) record A = Addr(s). Anyone inspecting both blockchains can link the Ethereum lock and the Parano1d lock for the same swap—including swaps that end in a refund. RPC metadata: Your Ethereum RPC endpoints see your IP address and Ethereum queries/broadcasts. Parano1d queries stay local on 127.0.0.1:9601 (talking to your own synced Parano1d node). Update check: Fetches release.json and release.json.sig from GitHub with no cookies, no auth token, and a generic user agent."},{"h":"Computer Awake & Power Caveats","a":"computer-awake-power-caveats","x":"No1d Swap holds an OS wake lock while coins are locked in a contract, and relaunches cleanly after unexpected crashes. macOS laptop lid caveat: Closing a MacBook lid on battery power can still force sleep even with an active wake lock. Keep laptops plugged in with the lid open until the swap reaches Done. Windows power cuts: Swap files are written before any transaction is broadcast (protecting against app crashes), but sudden power loss on Windows can still drop unflushed folder metadata."}]},{"t":"Live Track Record","u":"security/track-record/","s":[{"h":"Live Track Record","a":"","x":"We believe you should know exactly what has been tested on real networks versus what has only been tested offline. This page summarizes every real run recorded in 00Para/noid-swap up to release v0.1.8. Honest headline: 1 Ethereum mainnet swap has settled end-to-end (Run 8: 5 ↔ 0.001 , using the terminal swap tools). 3 two-machine desktop rehearsals (Windows + macOS installers, 4.10–4.12) have settled on Sepolia + Parano1d mainnet. 0 swaps between two separate people have run yet, and the desktop app's mainnet mode has not yet completed a live mainnet run (Run 23 stopped safely before any deposit due to the Parano1d miner-concentration screen)."},{"h":"Summary of Real Runs","a":"summary-of-real-runs","x":""},{"h":"Happy-Path & Mainnet Runs","a":"happy-path-mainnet-runs","x":"Run Network & Setup Amounts Outcome Runs 1–2 Parano1d mainnet + local Hardhat 10 ↔ 0.001 Settled (Run 1) and clean mutual refund when Alice never revealed (Run 2). Runs 3–4 Parano1d mainnet + public Sepolia (SHA-256 + ZK equiv) 10 ↔ 0.001 Settled in 44 min (Run 3); clean Alice recovery when Bob never locked (Run 4). Runs 5–6 Parano1d mainnet + public Sepolia (poseidon, proof-free) 10 ↔ 0.001 Settled in 47 min (Run 5); clean mutual refund after Bob's lock (Run 6). Run 8 Parano1d mainnet + Ethereum Mainnet (poseidon, production) 5 ↔ 0.001 Settled in 54 minutes from funding to both sides final (docs/live-run-8.md). Runs 9–14 Two machines + two Parano1d nodes (Sepolia, poseidon) 5 ↔ 0.001 Tested accidental triple-funding recovery (Run 9), post-lock abort (Run 10), two-machine happy path (Run 11: 44 min), two-node cross-check (Run 12: 45 min), operator console (Run 13: 50 min), and finality-18 update (Run 14: 51 m 49 s). Run 22 Swap's own wallet on Parano1d mainnet (one person, Alice's side only) 5 Verified funding, recovery close after D, and leftover sweeps (20,700 total fees). Run 23 Desktop app on Ethereum Mainnet — Stopped cleanly before any deposit by Bob's producer-concentration screen (docs/live-run-23.md)."},{"h":"Hostile-Counterparty Campaign (Runs 15–20, Sepolia + Parano1d Mainnet)","a":"hostile-counterparty-campaign-runs-1520-sepolia-parano1d-mainnet","x":"All six hostile scenarios were run live against real chains with one operator playing the misbehaving counterparty: Run Hostile Scenario Tested Result Run 15 (L1) Alice prepares and never funds Both sides aborted cleanly on their own; zero funds sent. Run 16 (L2) Alice under-funds (4 instead of agreed 5 ) Both tools refused the under-funded instance; Bob never locked; Alice recovered her 4 after D. Run 17 (L3) Bob crafts a short timelock (28 min left instead of 56 min) Alice's tool rejected the lock and refused to reveal; early refund rejected twice; both recovered cleanly. Run 18 (L4a) Reveal attempted 110 seconds past cutoff Tool refused the late reveal; safely fell back to mutual refund. Run 19 (L4b) Third party closes the contract before Bob using only public bundle + revealed s Settled in 49 m 41 s; close paid Bob's pinned payout address (4.994200 ). Proved trustless second-computer rescue live. Run 20 (L5/L6) Second Parano1d node disconnected at lock; epoch anchor rotation right before Bob's claim Lock refused while second node was down and succeeded once restored; claim waited across rotation and settled."},{"h":"Desktop App Two-Machine Rehearsals (4.10–4.12)","a":"desktop-app-two-machine-rehearsals-410412","x":"Setup: Packaged Windows installer (Alice) + macOS build (Bob), 5 ↔ 0.001 Sepolia , one approval per machine. Result: All three settled end-to-end (funding, lock, reveal, claim, and sweeps fully automated after single approval). Rehearsal 4.12 completed in 49 minutes and verified real-time detection of a short deposit (flagged 13 seconds after the block)."},{"h":"Automated Test Suite Coverage (Offline)","a":"automated-test-suite-coverage-offline","x":"In addition to live runs, the codebase ships with over 3,000 automated offline tests across all components (including deterministic chaos fuzzing and an explicit-state model checker): ui/app (console & desktop UI logic): 1,897 tests swap-cli (Rust state machine, watchers, wallets, chaos oracle, adversarial tests): 895 tests (+1 ignored) desktop (Electron hardening, vault, ACLs, self-test, updates): 636 tests scripts/live (shell helpers): 780 cases eth (Hardhat contract & live-helper tests): 562 tests btc (signet prototype, not packaged in desktop): 91 tests parano1d/harness & equiv (Pruv harness & reference parity): 78 tests (harness 38, equiv 40) Full minute-by-minute logs for Runs 1–23 are published in docs/live-run-*.md in 00Para/noid-swap."}]},{"t":"For Auditors & Cryptographers","u":"security/for-auditors/","s":[{"h":"For Auditors & Cryptographers","a":"","x":"No1d Swap has undergone extensive internal adversarial reviews—seven code reviews of swap-cli, two reviews of the Solidity Poseidon2b port, a protocol design review, an explicit-state model check, and a seeded chaos test—but zero independent external audits yet. If you are a security researcher, smart-contract auditor, or cryptographer looking to break No1d Swap, this page points you straight to the highest-impact attack surface."},{"h":"The Four Questions That Matter Most","a":"the-four-questions-that-matter-most","x":"In order of what rests on them (from docs/review-packet.md):"},{"h":"1. Preimage Resistance of Addr(s) as a Cross-Chain Hashlock","a":"1-preimage-resistance-of-addrs-as-a-cross-chain-hashlock","x":"The assumption: Both htlc.pruv (Parano1d) and HTLCPoseidonV2.sol (Ethereum) lock funds on A = Addr(s), which is a single Poseidon2b permutation over GF(2¹²⁸) using the designers' reference constants. What breaks if it fails: Anyone who can find any s' with Addr(s') = A can steal locked funds on both chains. Where to look: docs/parano1d-capabilities.md §10 (exact Addr definition & test vectors) vendor/parano1d/noid_poseidon2b/src/native/permutation.rs eth/contracts/Poseidon2b.sol & eth/contracts/HTLCPoseidonV2.sol"},{"h":"2. Mempool Pinning & Claim Stalling on Parano1d","a":"2-mempool-pinning-claim-stalling-on-parano1d","x":"The concern: Parano1d's mempool is first-seen per input with no replace-by-fee. Anyone who knows s (Alice from the start, everyone after reveal) can broadcast a minimum-fee close first. What to check: Is the 144-block epoch budget enough under network congestion, and does the fixed-fee contract variant (parano1d/contracts/htlc_v2.pruv, requiring fee == max_fee) cleanly eliminate the attack? Where to look: docs/review-1.md (R1) docs/htlc-v2-fixed-fee.md parano1d/contracts/htlc.pruv and parano1d/contracts/htlc_v2.pruv"},{"h":"3. Timeout Inequalities Under Reorgs & Fast Blocks","a":"3-timeout-inequalities-under-reorgs-fast-blocks","x":"The concern: Are the timeout margins (I1, I2, B_final = 19, D_offset = 738, W_A = 7,200 s, M_A = 1,800 s, bobOnlineMin = 75 min) sound under adverse block timing, reorgs, and producer concentration below 50%? Where to look: docs/protocol-design.md §1, §2.2, §4.2, §8, §9.1 swap-cli/src/params.rs (Gates::validate and profile constants) docs/formal/README.md, docs/formal/model.py, docs/formal/t21_race.py docs/miner-concentration.md and docs/bob-concentration-gate.md"},{"h":"4. Live Mainnet Surface vs. Experimental Code","a":"4-live-mainnet-surface-vs-experimental-code","x":"Focus your review on the live mainnet swap surface first (everything else is quarantined / test-only): Live Mainnet Surface Role eth/contracts/HTLCPoseidonV2.sol, eth/contracts/Poseidon2b.sol Mainnet HTLC (stack-only Yul Poseidon2b, pinned runtime code hash) parano1d/contracts/htlc.pruv, parano1d/harness/ Parano1d HTLC definition, opening builder, prover/verifier swap-cli/ State machine, watchers, wallet/key sealing, producer gate, claims eth/scripts/live.js, eth/scripts/bounded-send.js Ethereum transaction builder and bounded-send guard ui/app/, desktop/ Hardened Electron shell, vault, update verifier, local UI Not shipped on mainnet: btc/ (signet prototype), equiv/ (RISC Zero equivalence prover for legacy SHA-256 locks), eth/contracts/HTLCPoseidon.sol (early prototype with addrOf), eth/contracts/HTLC.sol, eth/contracts/HTLCERC20.sol, and the Nostr offer board."},{"h":"Fast-Start Reading Order for Reviewers","a":"fast-start-reading-order-for-reviewers","x":"docs/review-packet.md — single-document summary written specifically for external reviewers. docs/protocol-design.md (§1–§4, §8) — full protocol, timeout derivations, and threat model. swap-cli/REVIEW-7.md & docs/poseidon-v2-review.md — the latest internal adversarial findings and open items. docs/miner-concentration.md — empirical block-header measurements and race model."},{"h":"Running the Test Suites Locally","a":"running-the-test-suites-locally","x":"# Node suites (Node 21+, offline) (cd eth && npm install && npx hardhat test) node --test \"ui/app/test/*.test.js\" node --test \"desktop/test/*.test.js\" # Rust suites (Linux / macOS / WSL2), from the repository root (cd swap-cli && cargo test --offline) (cd parano1d/harness && cargo test)"},{"h":"Responsible Disclosure","a":"responsible-disclosure","x":"If you find a vulnerability affecting funds, do not publish it in a public issue. Open a minimal issue on 00Para/noid-swap saying only \"security, please open a private channel\" (or use GitHub private vulnerability reporting if enabled). Credit is given in the fix commit unless you prefer to stay anonymous."}]},{"t":"\"Something Needs a Look\" Playbook","u":"troubleshooting/common-issues/","s":[{"h":"\"Something Needs a Look\" Playbook","a":"","x":"No1d Swap is built to fail closed: whenever a node disagrees, a balance looks short, or a safety rule triggers, the app pauses and shows \"Something needs a look\" instead of guessing. That is normal and protects your coins. Find the message you see below and follow the exact steps."},{"h":"1. Miner Concentration Above 65% (Bob Cannot Approve)","a":"1-miner-concentration-above-65-bob-cannot-approve","x":"What happened: Parano1d's block headers show a single mining address produced more than 65% of blocks over the last 24 hours (2,880 blocks) or 6 hours (720 blocks). What to do: Nothing is broken and no coins were locked. This hard block cannot be overridden. Wait until block production spreads out and both windows drop back to 65% or lower—the approval screen reopens automatically."},{"h":"2. Short Deposit Flagged","a":"2-short-deposit-flagged","x":"What happened: You sent slightly less or to the swap's deposit address than required (usually because wallet fees or the gas buffer weren't included). What to do: Look at the exact shortfall amount shown on the swap card. Send only the missing difference to the same swap deposit address shown on screen. Within ~15–60 seconds of the top-up block, the app automatically picks up the full balance and continues."},{"h":"3. Stuck Reveal or Stuck ETH Refund (Gas Spike)","a":"3-stuck-reveal-or-stuck-eth-refund-gas-spike","x":"What happened: Ethereum base fees rose above the fee cap you set (default 1 gwei), so your reveal or refund transaction is sitting unconfirmed in the mempool. What to do: Do not restart or delete the swap. Use the card's re-send at a higher fee cap action to replace the stuck transaction with a higher max fee. For Alice: Check the clock carefully—your app automatically refuses to reveal in the last 30 minutes before Bob's refund opens. If you bump the fee before the cutoff, the replacement goes out immediately."},{"h":"4. \"Ended Before It Started\" (or Held Coins in an Unstarted Swap)","a":"4-ended-before-it-started-or-held-coins-in-an-unstarted-swap","x":"What happened: The funding or lock deadline passed before either side locked on-chain. If the swap wallets hold zero balance: The app automatically moves the swap under Ended as \"Ended before it started.\" Nothing was sent and nothing is needed from you. If you already sent coins to the swap wallet before it expired: The app never quietly hides a swap that holds funds. It stays active with Needs you, displays \"Held by this swap\" under Details, and shows \"Do not send anything here\" + \"Read the wallet again.\" Let the app record the abort and sweep your coins back to your payout address."},{"h":"5. Stale Node Reading or Two Nodes Disagree","a":"5-stale-node-reading-or-two-nodes-disagree","x":"What happened: Your local Parano1d node (127.0.0.1:9601) fell behind, disconnected, or your two configured nodes reported different block tips, timestamps, or lock states. What to do: Open the Parano1d wallet app and confirm it is running and synced to the latest block. Check your internet connection and Ethereum RPC endpoints in Settings. Wait for two clean, fresh readings—the app resumes automatically once both nodes agree. Never ignore a node-disagreement alarm on a live swap—if your primary and secondary nodes genuinely disagree on a block hash or producer, stop and check chain explorers/peers before locking."},{"h":"6. Bob's Late-Claim Alarm","a":"6-bobs-late-claim-alarm","x":"What happened: Alice has already revealed s on Ethereum, and the remaining time before Parano1d deadline D - 19 is getting short. What to do immediately: Make sure your Parano1d wallet app is open, synced, and responding on 127.0.0.1:9601. Keep your computer plugged in and online—your app will broadcast the close automatically as soon as it can reach a synced node. If this computer cannot sync or reach Parano1d in time, immediately use a second computer to broadcast Bob's close. See Emergency Recovery & Second-Computer Close."},{"h":"7. Leftover Sweep Waiting / Delayed","a":"7-leftover-sweep-waiting-delayed","x":"What happened: The main swap settled, or aborted cleanly, and one side's leftover sweep (e.g., dust or remaining gas) hasn't gone out yet. What to do: If the swap wallet had no coin larger than the sweep fee (~0.0058 or 21,000 gas), there may simply be nothing worth sweeping left—the app retries on a 5-minute timer while letting the other chain's sweep finish immediately. Check the balances under Details. Once the main payout arrived in your own address, your swap coins are safe."},{"h":"8. \"This Swap Cannot End by Itself\" (Prepared Intent Never Sent)","a":"8-this-swap-cannot-end-by-itself-prepared-intent-never-sent","x":"What happened: Rarely, a swap records an intent to fund or lock right before a crash or interruption, and the app cannot prove on its own whether that transaction was ever sent. What to do: Your coins are safe inside the swap's own wallet. Follow the Cancel a Never-Sent Intent procedure in Emergency Recovery & Second-Computer Close."},{"h":"9. \"This Installation Is Damaged: Reinstall It.\"","a":"9-this-installation-is-damaged-reinstall-it","x":"What happened: At startup, the packaged app's runtime manifest check failed (corrupted disk, antivirus quarantine of a runtime binary, or tampered install). What to do: Do not delete your data folder (runs/ is separate from the app install folder and is never deleted by uninstalling). Re-download and verify the installer using Download & Verify the Installer, then reinstall. If you have an urgent active swap and cannot reinstall immediately, use your paper recovery code + swap-cli via Emergency Recovery & Second-Computer Close."}]},{"t":"Emergency Recovery & Second-Computer Close","u":"troubleshooting/recovery-and-second-computer/","s":[{"h":"Emergency Recovery & Second-Computer Close","a":"","x":"Even if the desktop app crashes, refuses to open, or gets uninstalled mid-swap, your coins are never trapped inside the UI. Everything lives in standard swap files inside your data folder and can be finished with swap-cli alone."},{"h":"Part 1: Open a Swap by Hand (With Your Recovery Code)","a":"part-1-open-a-swap-by-hand-with-your-recovery-code","x":"Use this procedure if the No1d Swap desktop app will not start and you need to finish a refund, claim, or sweep directly with swap-cli."},{"h":"What You Need","a":"what-you-need","x":"Your paper recovery code (shown on first launch). The swap's complete run folder (runs/<swap-id>/): macOS: ~/Library/Application Support/No1d Swap/runs/<swap-id> Windows: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\No1d Swap\\runs\\<swap-id> (Uninstalling No1d Swap never deletes this folder.) swap-cli: either the copy shipped inside the app (No1d Swap.app/Contents/Resources/bin/swap-cli on macOS, resources\\bin\\swap-cli.exe on Windows) or built from source. A copy of the public repository (00Para/noid-swap) and Node.js 22+."},{"h":"Derive the Swap Passphrase Safely","a":"derive-the-swap-passphrase-safely","x":"Run in Terminal (macOS) or Git Bash (Windows): REPO=/path/to/noid-swap RUN=\"/path/to/runs/<swap-id>\" export SWAP_PASSPHRASE=\"$(node \"$REPO/desktop/tools/recovery.js\" passphrase \"$RUN\")\" # Type your paper recovery code, then press Enter and Ctrl+D # (In a native Windows console window: Ctrl+Z, then Enter) Why stdin? recovery.js only reads your recovery code from standard input—never from a command-line argument—so it stays out of your shell history and process list. It also checks vault-check.json to make sure the code matches that run folder. Once SWAP_PASSPHRASE is exported in that shell, every swap-cli command for that swap unlocks its files automatically (see swap-cli/README.md for wallet and key commands). Close the terminal window when finished so SWAP_PASSPHRASE is cleared from memory."},{"h":"Part 2: Cancel a Never-Sent Intent","a":"part-2-cancel-a-never-sent-intent","x":"If the app warns that a swap prepared a funding or lock that was never sent and cannot end on its own: First verify on-chain (in your Parano1d wallet app, or on Etherscan/Sepolia) that no funding or lock transaction for this swap was actually broadcast. Open the swap in a terminal using Part 1 above so SWAP_PASSPHRASE is set. Run the explicit cancellation command with the same node flags your swap uses: # For Alice: swap-cli intent \"$RUN/alice.swap.json\" --cancel --i-checked-the-wallet <NODE_OPTIONS> # For Bob: swap-cli intent \"$RUN/bob/bob.swap.json\" --cancel --i-checked-the-wallet <NODE_OPTIONS> Reopen No1d Swap. The app reads both chains again, marks the swap ended, and sweeps any funds left in the swap wallet back to your own payout address."},{"h":"Part 3: Close Bob's NOID Swap From a Second Computer","a":"part-3-close-bobs-noid-swap-from-a-second-computer","x":"Trustless rescue for Bob: Once Alice takes your on Ethereum, the secret s is public on-chain. From that moment on, anyone with the public swap files can broadcast the close—and the Parano1d contract forces 100% of the payout strictly to Bob's pinned Parano1d address. No , no , and no wallet key is required on the second computer (the ~0.0058 close fee comes out of the locked ). Use this if you are Bob, Alice has already revealed s, and your main computer is off, loses internet, or cannot sync Parano1d in time before D - 19. Shown live with the terminal tools, not yet with the desktop app. A third-party close paid Bob's address in run 19 (docs/live-run-19.md), using the terminal helpers of scripts/live/. Nobody has yet done it from a copy of a desktop app's run folder. Read docs/runbook-two-party.md before you need it, not during the swap."},{"h":"What the Second Computer Needs","a":"what-the-second-computer-needs","x":"The project's terminal tools set up once with scripts/live/setup.sh. A synced Parano1d node. A copy of this swap's folder (bob.swap.json and Alice's public bundle alice.bundle.json). Privacy note: Your Ethereum swap key inside bob.swap.json stays sealed without your passphrase, but keep the copied folder private and delete it from the second computer once finished."},{"h":"Steps on the Second Computer","a":"steps-on-the-second-computer","x":"Run one tick to read Alice's revealed secret s from Ethereum. Run a dry-run of claim.sh and verify that the recipient address printed on screen is your own Parano1d address. Broadcast the claim: claim.sh --submit"},{"h":"Back on Your Main Computer","a":"back-on-your-main-computer","x":"Reopen No1d Swap and open the swap. Until you record it, the swap will show \"Something needs a look\" (your is already safely in your own wallet either way). Click Record a close made elsewhere. The app verifies the close on-chain before marking the swap settled."}]},{"t":"Frequently Asked Questions","u":"reference/faq/","s":[{"h":"Frequently Asked Questions","a":"","x":""},{"h":"Why is there no bridge, liquidity pool, or wrapped NOID?","a":"why-is-there-no-bridge-liquidity-pool-or-wrapped-noid","x":"Bridges and pools introduce custodial multisigs, smart-contract liquidity honeypots, and admin keys. No1d Swap locks real on Parano1d and real on Ethereum directly between two people. Neither contract has an owner, admin key, or fee."},{"h":"Is there a fee to use No1d Swap?","a":"is-there-a-fee-to-use-no1d-swap","x":"No. The project takes 0%. You pay only standard Ethereum gas and Parano1d network transaction fees. If you'd like to support development and an external security audit voluntarily, donations go to o1epu2t45yg2a87xfvtzd3yuha8vejj9k4s762n89dwun3cp67k7nqkt2ued."},{"h":"Is Sepolia NOID fake test money?","a":"is-sepolia-noid-fake-test-money","x":"No! Parano1d has no test network. When you select Sepolia in No1d Swap, the Ethereum side uses free Sepolia test , but the side is always real Parano1d mainnet (capped at 20 per swap on Sepolia). If you want to try the app with zero real coins, use the built-in Practice Swap from the app menu."},{"h":"Why does the NOID seller (Alice) always have to lock first?","a":"why-does-the-noid-seller-alice-always-have-to-lock-first","x":"Parano1d uses zero-knowledge proofs instead of signatures, so spending never reveals a secret on-chain. The secret can only be revealed on Ethereum—which means the person who creates the secret (Alice, selling ) must lock first with the longer deadline and claim to publish the secret for Bob. See How It Works."},{"h":"How long does a swap take?","a":"how-long-does-a-swap-take","x":"Normal swap: About 50 minutes from Alice's funding to both sides finalized (most of this is waiting for ~19 minutes of Ethereum finality and ~10 minutes of Parano1d finality). If the swap stops mid-way: Bob's returns about 2 h 20 min after his lock; Alice's returns about 6 h 45 min after Alice presses Prepare."},{"h":"Can I close my laptop or quit the app mid-swap?","a":"can-i-close-my-laptop-or-quit-the-app-mid-swap","x":"Keep it open and plugged in. While coins are locked, No1d Swap keeps your computer awake automatically—except on macOS laptops, where closing the lid on battery can still put the Mac to sleep. If you accidentally quit the app or your computer restarts, reopen No1d Swap and it will pick up where it left off from your saved run folder. Important for Bob: Deadlines keep ticking on the blockchains even if your computer is off. After Alice reveals the secret on Ethereum, Bob's computer must be online within ~75 minutes to claim his (or use a second computer)."},{"h":"Why did Windows or macOS warn me when installing?","a":"why-did-windows-or-macos-warn-me-when-installing","x":"No1d Swap is an independent open-source project with no corporate entity and no commercial Apple/Microsoft code-signing certificate. OS warnings are expected—do not bypass them until you have cryptographically verified release.json.sig and the installer's SHA-256 using Download & Verify the Installer."},{"h":"Why are swaps capped at 0.5 ETH and 2,500 NOID?","a":"why-are-swaps-capped-at-05-eth-and-2500-noid","x":"No1d Swap is young software and has not had an independent external audit yet. The hard caps protect early users from putting large balances into unaudited code and reflect current Parano1d mining concentration."},{"h":"Why is Bob's approval blocked with a miner-concentration message?","a":"why-is-bobs-approval-blocked-with-a-miner-concentration-message","x":"Before Bob locks , his app reads who mined the last 24 hours and 6 hours of Parano1d blocks. If a single mining address produced more than 65% of blocks in either window, No1d Swap hard-blocks new locks until mining decentralizes back to ≤ 65%. See Limits & Risks."},{"h":"Can I use a Safe / Gnosis / smart-contract wallet as my Ethereum payout address?","a":"can-i-use-a-safe-gnosis-smart-contract-wallet-as-my-ethereum-payout-address","x":"No. Use a standard externally owned account (EOA), such as a normal MetaMask or hardware-wallet address. A smart-contract wallet that reverts on receiving could cause Alice's claim() transaction to fail after exposing the secret in calldata."},{"h":"Does No1d Swap support USDC, Bitcoin, or an order book?","a":"does-no1d-swap-support-usdc-bitcoin-or-an-order-book","x":"Not on mainnet today. The repository contains prototypes for Bitcoin (btc/, signet-only), ERC-20 (HTLCERC20.sol), and a Nostr offer board (test networks only), all intentionally disabled on mainnet to keep the launch surface minimal and verifiable."},{"h":"What if I lose my computer—can my recovery code restore everything by itself?","a":"what-if-i-lose-my-computercan-my-recovery-code-restore-everything-by-itself","x":"Your paper recovery code unlocks your swap files when combined with your swap's run folder (runs/<swap-id>/). It is not a BIP-39 seed phrase that scans the blockchain—keep your paper recovery code safe and back up any active run folder if you have funds currently locked."}]},{"t":"Glossary","u":"reference/glossary/","s":[{"h":"Glossary","a":"","x":"Plain-English definitions of every term you'll see in No1d Swap and these docs."},{"h":"Alice","a":"alice","x":"The person selling and receiving . Alice generates the swap secret, locks first with the longer deadline (~6 h 45 min if stopped), and reveals the secret on Ethereum to claim Bob's ."},{"h":"Bob","a":"bob","x":"The person selling and receiving . Bob creates the invitation first, locks second with the shorter deadline (~2 h 20 min if stopped), reads Alice's revealed secret from Ethereum, and claims before the Parano1d deadline."},{"h":"Check Line","a":"check-line","x":"A 16-digit verification code formatted as four groups of four digits (xxxx xxxx xxxx xxxx). Both computers derive it independently from the exact swap terms. Alice and Bob compare it over a second channel before approving so neither side can be tricked by a typo or tampered file."},{"h":"Close (Parano1d)","a":"close-parano1d","x":"The transaction that terminates a Parano1d HashTimeLock contract and sends its locked to either Bob (before deadline D, with a proof of knowing s) or Alice (at or after deadline D). The close fee (at least 5,800 , capped at max_fee) is paid from the locked ."},{"h":"Deadline (D and T_reveal)","a":"deadline-d-and-treveal","x":"The exact block height (D on Parano1d, ~738 blocks after Prepare) or timestamp (T_reveal on Ethereum, 7,200 seconds after Bob locks) after which a lock switches from claimable by the counterparty to refundable by the original sender."},{"h":"EOA (Externally Owned Account)","a":"eoa-externally-owned-account","x":"A normal Ethereum wallet controlled by a private key (e.g., hardware wallet or standard MetaMask account)—as opposed to a smart-contract wallet. No1d Swap requires EOAs for Ethereum payout addresses."},{"h":"Finality","a":"finality","x":"The point at which a blockchain considers a block irreversible under normal operation. No1d Swap waits for finality before taking the next step: ~19 minutes (1,140 seconds) on Ethereum and 19 blocks (~9.5 minutes; is_final at 18 blocks after inclusion) on Parano1d."},{"h":"Hash-Time Lock (HTLC)","a":"hash-time-lock-htlc","x":"A smart contract lock with two paths: before a deadline, whoever proves knowledge of a secret s matching the hashlock gets the coins; after the deadline, the original sender gets them back."},{"h":"Invitation (no1d-invitation.json / no1dinv1:…)","a":"invitation-no1d-invitationjson-no1dinv1","x":"The public swap parameters Bob sends to Alice to start a swap (network, contract, amount, amount, and Bob's two public addresses). Holds zero secrets. A JSON file, and since v0.1.8 also a one-line no1dinv1:… code with a checksum that catches a damaged paste (not a forged one: Alice still checks every filled value and the check line)."},{"h":"Micro-NOID (μNOID)","a":"micro-noid-μnoid","x":"The smallest unit of : 1 = 1,000,000 . Parano1d close fees start at 5,800 (0.0058 )."},{"h":"Poseidon2b (Addr(s))","a":"poseidon2b-addrs","x":"Parano1d's native hash function over GF(2¹²⁸) that derives a 32-byte Parano1d address from a 32-byte secret s. Because HTLCPoseidonV2 implements Poseidon2b directly on Ethereum, both chains lock on the exact same 32-byte value A = Addr(s) without needing an off-chain ZK equivalence proof."},{"h":"Producer Share (Miner Concentration)","a":"producer-share-miner-concentration","x":"The percentage of recent Parano1d blocks mined by a single address over the last 24 hours (2,880 blocks) and 6 hours (720 blocks). Checked automatically before Bob locks (explicit approval above 35%, hard block above 65%)."},{"h":"Recovery Code","a":"recovery-code","x":"The paper backup code displayed on first launch. Combined with a swap's run folder (runs/<swap-id>/), it lets you derive the swap passphrase offline and finish or recover a swap directly with swap-cli if the desktop app won't start."},{"h":"Reveal","a":"reveal","x":"The Ethereum transaction in which Alice calls HTLCPoseidonV2.claim(lockId, s), collecting Bob's and publishing the secret s on-chain in the Claimed event so Bob's app can read it and claim the ."},{"h":"Reveal Cutoff (M_A)","a":"reveal-cutoff-ma","x":"The 30-minute safety buffer before T_reveal during which Alice's app refuses to broadcast a reveal. Prevents Alice from publishing s so late that Bob refunds his while still learning s."},{"h":"Run Folder","a":"run-folder","x":"The private directory storing a swap's state and sealed files (~/Library/Application Support/No1d Swap/runs/<swap-id> on macOS, %LOCALAPPDATA%\\No1d Swap\\runs\\<swap-id> on Windows). Never deleted when you uninstall the app."},{"h":"Sweep","a":"sweep","x":"The automatic final transaction that moves any leftover change or unused gas out of a swap's temporary wallets back into your own payout addresses once the swap ends."},{"h":"Swap Copy / Public Bundle (alice.bundle.json)","a":"swap-copy-public-bundle-alicebundlejson","x":"The public file Alice saves after pressing Prepare and sends to Bob. Contains the hashlock, contract parameters, and public terms (zero secrets) so Bob's app can independently verify everything and compute the matching 16-digit check line."}]}];
